1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,523
Aarav shouts into a cheap microphone.

2
00:00:02,823 --> 00:00:06,224
On the screen, the tops of
his voice wave look cut flat.

3
00:00:06,524 --> 00:00:07,424
Who cut them?

4
00:00:07,277 --> 00:00:08,997
Circuits can do this on purpose too.

5
00:00:09,497 --> 00:00:11,657
Two simple diode circuits change a wave.

6
00:00:11,957 --> 00:00:15,942
A clipper cuts off the part of
a wave above or below a level.

7
00:00:16,242 --> 00:00:18,909
Think of a low ceiling over a jumping kid.

8
00:00:18,909 --> 00:00:20,755
The high jumps get flattened.

9
00:00:21,055 --> 00:00:25,239
A clamper lifts or lowers the whole
wave, without changing its shape.

10
00:00:25,539 --> 00:00:28,185
Think of putting the
trampoline on a stage.

11
00:00:28,185 --> 00:00:29,876
Same jumps, higher floor.

12
00:00:30,476 --> 00:00:32,715
Let's build a clipper on the breadboard.

13
00:00:33,015 --> 00:00:37,465
An AC source, a resistor in series,
and a diode across the output.

14
00:00:37,815 --> 00:00:41,483
The scope shows the input in
grey and the output in amber.

15
00:00:41,833 --> 00:00:45,132
In the negative half, the
diode is reverse biased.

16
00:00:45,132 --> 00:00:47,088
It acts like an open switch.

17
00:00:47,438 --> 00:00:49,742
So the output simply follows the input.

18
00:00:50,092 --> 00:00:54,329
In the positive half, the input
crosses zero point seven volts.

19
00:00:54,329 --> 00:00:55,796
The diode turns on.

20
00:00:56,146 --> 00:01:00,214
Now the diode holds the output
at about zero point seven volts.

21
00:01:00,564 --> 00:01:04,017
The extra voltage drops
across the resistor instead.

22
00:01:04,367 --> 00:01:05,790
The top is cut off.

23
00:01:05,790 --> 00:01:07,934
This is a positive shunt clipper.

24
00:01:08,534 --> 00:01:11,878
Flip the diode, and it cuts
the negative half instead.

25
00:01:12,478 --> 00:01:14,315
Want a different cut level?

26
00:01:14,315 --> 00:01:17,254
Add a small battery in
series with the diode.

27
00:01:17,604 --> 00:01:21,930
Now the diode turns on only when the
input beats the battery plus zero

28
00:01:21,930 --> 00:01:22,886
point seven.

29
00:01:23,586 --> 00:01:27,281
So the clip level is VB
plus zero point seven volts.

30
00:01:27,981 --> 00:01:32,526
With a three volt battery, the wave
is cut at three point seven volts.

31
00:01:33,126 --> 00:01:36,735
Clippers protect circuits from
sudden spikes, and they shape waves.

32
00:01:37,335 --> 00:01:38,532
Now the clamper.

33
00:01:38,532 --> 00:01:41,589
It needs a capacitor,
a diode and a resistor.

34
00:01:41,889 --> 00:01:43,936
The capacitor sits in series.

35
00:01:43,936 --> 00:01:46,250
The diode sits across the output.

36
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:50,049
In the first negative
half, the diode conducts.

37
00:01:50,049 --> 00:01:53,065
The capacitor charges to the peak, V m.

38
00:01:53,415 --> 00:01:57,916
After that, the capacitor acts
like a small battery of V m volts.

39
00:01:58,266 --> 00:02:01,074
It adds V m to every point of the input.

40
00:02:01,424 --> 00:02:03,366
Watch the output trace lift.

41
00:02:03,366 --> 00:02:05,725
Its lowest point now sits at zero.

42
00:02:06,325 --> 00:02:08,825
The peak to peak size stays the same.

43
00:02:08,825 --> 00:02:10,317
Only the level moves.

44
00:02:10,917 --> 00:02:12,812
This is a positive clamper.

45
00:02:12,812 --> 00:02:15,375
Flip the diode for a negative clamper.

46
00:02:15,975 --> 00:02:20,883
RC must be much larger than the
period, so the capacitor stays charged.

47
00:02:21,483 --> 00:02:23,596
Clampers are also called DC restorers.

48
00:02:24,196 --> 00:02:25,862
A clipper cuts the wave.

49
00:02:25,862 --> 00:02:27,576
A clamper lifts the wave.

50
00:02:28,376 --> 00:02:29,479
Worked example.

51
00:02:29,479 --> 00:02:33,537
A sine wave of peak five volts
feeds an ideal positive clamper.

52
00:02:33,887 --> 00:02:37,310
The capacitor charges
to the peak, five volts.

53
00:02:37,660 --> 00:02:39,741
Output equals input plus five.

54
00:02:39,741 --> 00:02:42,878
The lowest point goes
from minus five to zero.

55
00:02:43,578 --> 00:02:46,238
The highest point goes from five to ten.

56
00:02:46,238 --> 00:02:49,151
So the output swings
from zero to ten volts.

57
00:02:49,801 --> 00:02:51,262
Now, the exam trap.

58
00:02:51,612 --> 00:02:54,644
Many students think a clamper
changes the shape of the wave.

59
00:02:54,994 --> 00:02:55,910
It does not.

60
00:02:55,910 --> 00:02:57,831
A clamper only shifts the wave.

61
00:02:57,831 --> 00:02:59,565
A clipper changes the shape.

62
00:03:00,215 --> 00:03:01,348
Where is it asked?

63
00:03:01,348 --> 00:03:04,707
PUC board papers ask you to draw
the circuit and its waveforms.

64
00:03:05,057 --> 00:03:09,286
JEE Main asks for clipped diode
outputs, as in June twenty twenty two.

65
00:03:09,936 --> 00:03:10,836
Try this.

66
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:13,729
A ten volt peak sine wave
feeds a shunt clipper.

67
00:03:13,729 --> 00:03:17,139
A silicon diode is in series
with a three volt battery.

68
00:03:17,139 --> 00:03:18,909
What are the output limits?

69
00:03:24,309 --> 00:03:25,209
B.

70
00:03:24,692 --> 00:03:27,718
Plus three point seven
volts and minus ten volts.

71
00:03:28,068 --> 00:03:32,222
The diode turns on when the output
tries to rise above three plus zero

72
00:03:32,222 --> 00:03:33,139
point seven.

73
00:03:33,489 --> 00:03:36,358
So the top is held at
three point seven volts.

74
00:03:36,708 --> 00:03:39,327
In the negative half, the diode is off.

75
00:03:39,327 --> 00:03:42,286
The output follows the
input down to minus ten.

76
00:03:42,936 --> 00:03:44,482
Screenshot this card.

77
00:03:47,682 --> 00:03:50,226
Next, the bipolar junction transistor.

78
00:03:50,226 --> 00:03:53,359
How do three thin layers
control a big current?
